  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $4$th term is $324$ and the common ratio is $3?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 324 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{(4-1)} \\\\ 324 & =27{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=12$ and $r=3.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{12(1-{3}^{N})}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-{3}^{N})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-6(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & =6({3}^{N}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
